# The UI contract comes first: the model, then the screen, then the code

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: leaf (presentation, tests, the modeling thread), reached from `process-check-rules-skills.mdc`; its procedure is the skill `.agents/skills/adding-ui-anchors/SKILL.md`.

## Use This When

- A view gains, renames or loses an anchor, a code, a state, a choice value, a control, a list, a dialog or a screen.
- A file under `contract/` or `tests/e2e/scenarios/` changes, an e2e test names the contract, or a test or an agent cannot find an element.

## Stop Here If

- `bun run contract:check`, `contract:check:sources`, `contract:check:consumers` and `e2e:coverage` are green, `bun run contract:coverage --unmarked` lists no unmarked control, and the scenario that reaches the element has its test.

## Descend To

- The steps, the patterns of our kit and views, the checklists and the templates: `.agents/skills/adding-ui-anchors/SKILL.md` and its `references/`. The closed lists: `contract/vocabulary.md`, never copied. The folder for people: `contract/README.md`.
- Visible text and `aria-label` keys: `ui-i18n-direct-keys.mdc`. The controls: `ui-primitive-hygiene.mdc`.

## Core rule

- **Model first.** Completeness comes from `contract/domain/model.schema.json`: a screen unfolds its entities, the anchors bind their fields and actions, and the DOM, the tests and the scenarios are checked back against the contract. A change starts in the model, then the screen file, then the scenario, then the code and the tests; an anchor, a code, a state or a route never appears first in JSX or in a test.
- **Who decides.** The developer programs the architecture in the contract, apart from the implementation: the model, the screens with their flows, the platform, the activities and the scenarios, every architectural decision taken there. The agent brings the patterns that make the contract good, proposed for the developer's review with their trade-offs, and builds the implementation from the contract the developer approved. The code takes no architectural decision of its own: a question the code raises goes back to the contract and its review.
- **Two layers, three kinds of commit.** The contract has a business layer and a meta layer. The business layer is the entry point of every business feature: `contract/domain/` (the screens, the activities, the platform, the model), the scenarios of `tests/e2e/scenarios/`, and the content tables of `contract/vocabulary.md` (the codes, the states, the choice values, the screen names, the roles, the shell's reserved anchors): a row of them travels with the screen file that first uses it. The meta layer is the engine that keeps the contract working: `@workspace/ui-contract` (its schemas, checks, manifest, runtime helpers, and `./machines`, the walk that runs every flow and activity in XState inside `contract:check` and the comparison an app holds its own machines to their flows with), the generated `contract/schemas/`, the grammar of `contract/vocabulary.md` (its Grammar section, the kinds, the derived suffixes, the attributes, the contract version), `contract/README.md`, the rules and skills about the contract, the contract steps of the pre-commit hook, the prose of `COVERAGE.md` and the contract's rows of `docs/agents/context-smoke-test.md`. An app is one consumer of the engine, a particular case: its code and its tests are the implementation, its runtime of the contract (`window.__perch.ui`, the served manifest) and the specs that hold it to the contract included (the web app's `apps/web/src/platform/machines.spec.ts` with its `APP_MACHINES`, `routes-*.spec.ts`, the e2e spec of the manifest), and so is an example in a meta doc that quotes the app's code, which changes in the implementation commit that changes that code. A meta commit touches an app only where the app consumes what the engine changed (an import path, a call), never what the app does. On a branch a commit holds one of the three, never two. A feature that needs a new facet lands the meta change first (the engine learns it), then commits the business change that uses it. The generated block of `COVERAGE.md` goes with whichever commit changed what it measures.
- **Branches**, by `branch-reuse-first.mdc`, whose example this is. The meta layer's area is the contract engine, and its branch is `feat/contract-first-pass`: a new round first merges the latest `main` into it, then takes its meta commits, and the branch lands on `main` as a squash of its own after the developer's go on its hand-over, which names the meta commits by hash and the business change that will use them, with the engine's specs showing the facet in use. The go covers exactly those commits: one round is open at a time, and a meta commit added after the hand-over, another feature's included, needs a new hand-over of the whole round before the squash. A change that hand-over's review brings is, as in the business review below, a commit of its own on top, never an amend or a re-cut. A feature's business commit (the review stop below) and then its implementation commits go on the feature's own branch, with `main` merged in once the meta it needs has landed there, and that branch lands on `main` as its own squash. A facet found missing in the middle of a feature takes the same road: its meta goes on `feat/contract-first-pass` and lands, `main` is merged into the feature branch (the contract commits already approved are never rebased), and the business commit that uses it stops for review.
- **Review gate.** A change to what the app shows or does runs in two phases. First the contract: the business layer (the model, the screen file with its anchors, facets and flow's states, events, guards and effects, the platform, the activities and the scenarios), on the feature branch, after the meta layer has landed when a facet is new. A behaviour goes into that structure, never into the prose of a `description`; one the contract cannot say is signalled (`contract-gap-no-emulation.mdc`). Then stop and hand the developer the contract diff (`bun run contract:diff main`, the diff of those commits named by hash, the meta landing they build on, the reason for each change, the gaps signalled), ask for the OK on those commits, and wait. The implementation (the code, its tests and the e2e tests, their scaffold included, and the docs that quote the code) starts only after the developer's OK, in commits of its own. The OK covers exactly the contract commits it was given for, and the one commit it approves in advance: the last business commit of a rename or a removal, which drops the old names the review listed and nothing else. A change the review brings, one the developer asks for or one an answer to their question leads to, is a commit of its own on top of the ones under review, never an amend or a re-cut, so the developer follows each decision as its own step; the OK is then asked on all of them. Any other contract commit made, re-cut, amended or rebased after the OK, even with its files unchanged, and a return to the implementation after other work, stop again, and no commit of the implementation stays on the branch without an OK on the contract commits beneath it; a contract change found during the implementation is a commit of its own and goes back to the first phase and its stop. The gate holds whenever the intended behaviour changes or the contract needs a correction or an extension; a fix that brings the code back to what the contract already says, correctly, needs no contract diff and no stop. A screen's transient state comes from its flow run as its machine, never from a component's own flags.
- **The files.** Authored: `contract/vocabulary.md`, `contract/domain/screens/<pathname>.yaml` (`__root.yaml` for the frame), `contract/domain/platform.yaml`, `contract/domain/model.schema.json`, `contract/domain/activities/<id>.yaml`. Generated, never edited: `screen.schema.json`, `platform.schema.json`, `activity.schema.json`, `x-perch.schema.json`, `manifest.schema.json` (`bun run contract:schemas`) and the block of `contract/COVERAGE.md`. A word the vocabulary lacks is added to it in the same change. A platform action that sends a mail declares it (`mail`: `to`, `language`, `link`); scenarios name it `[mail:<action>]`, and a test holds a mail it reads to it. A mail that only tells, with no token, is a notice the action lists under `notices` (`name`, `to`, `language`, an optional plain `link` to a screen whose routes need no parameter), named `[notice:<action>.<name>]`.
- **Grammar.** An anchor (`data-testid`) is 2 to 4 dot-separated kebab-case segments, a screen name first and a kind last (`user.sign-in.email-input`); it says what the element is for, never its text, colour, position or an entity's id.
- **One literal per rendered name**, declared once in one screen file and written once in the sources; no anchor equals another plus a derived suffix (`device.list.session-item`, never `device.list.item`). Four literal forms only: `data-testid="..."`, a `testId` or `<part>TestId` prop, a static `testId: '...'` key, a `<NAME>_TEST_ID` constant; never built at runtime.
- **Derived names only through `testIdFor`.** A composite takes one `testId` and builds its parts with `testIdFor(testId, '<suffix>')`, the suffix from the vocabulary and in the base's `derived`; a part with a screen-level name of its own is a named prop (`navigationTestId`).
- **Placement.** On the semantic element whose role the kind maps to, never on a wrapper, a fragment, a portal or a backdrop; a leaf kit component spreads its props, so the call site writes the literal.
- **What needs one.** A container that scopes a test, every entry of a list, every control a test or an agent drives (every label is translated), a value a test reads, every state and message; not decorations, wrappers or an icon inside a marked control. A control that is not a test target carries a coverage-ignore marker, never a made-up anchor; an icon-only control also carries an `aria-label`.
- **Codes, states and values, never wording.** An error's `fields` name the field each of its codes concerns, which it shows under and marks invalid. `data-code` on a `message`, `error`, `toast` or `dialog` (a dialog's code names what it asks), `data-state` on a container or an entry, `data-value` with the key of a choice that is not an entity. A behaviour that varies is a code or a state, never a second anchor; a domain code is mapped to a contract code in the presentation layer. A field that holds a secret (a password, its confirmation) says `secret: true`, never travels in an address (no navigate's search carries it, no search parameter starts it), and alone may render a `visibility-button`. A field holds input unless its own change runs its action, which the loader derives (`holdsInput`); the element of a field that holds none carries `holdsInput={false}`, and the sources check holds it to the contract. Every leave that would lose unsaved input is declared in `platform.yaml` under `unsavedInput` with how it asks (through the frame's dialog, by the browser, never, or it cannot); the browser's own prompt is drawn outside the page and has no anchor.
- **Repeated items** share one static anchor with `data-id`; an entry that differs or changes in place carries a row state, a normal one none. **A composite mounted on its own is not a singleton**: the shell's instance owns the reserved anchor, every other mount passes its own `testId`, and a feature never renders a reserved anchor.
- **Scenarios** are Markdown files, `tests/e2e/scenarios/<entity>/<layer>/[<area>/]<slug>.md` beside `tests/e2e/src/<same path>.e2e.spec.ts`, whose brackets name what the contract declares (an anchor, a route, `[code:<code>]`, `[state:<state>]`, `[value:<value>]`, `[mail:<action>]`, `[notice:<action>.<name>]`), and the contract's feedback loop: a scenario that cannot be written from the contract alone names a gap to fix in the contract files, never one to work around in a test; a gap the contract cannot close in the change is signalled, never emulated (`contract-gap-no-emulation.mdc`).
- **Routes.** A binding is a TanStack Router route and a state address; `apps/web/src/platform/routes-contract.spec.ts` and `routes-router.spec.ts` hold the module routes and the real router to it, and `routes-examples.spec.ts` its example link (`bun run contract:routes`) and the route tree.
- **Flows.** A screen's `flow` is an XState v5 machine config in the subset `setup({ guards, actions, actors }).createMachine(flow)` takes as written (the route owns the addressable state, the machine the transient one, save whether a password input shows what it holds, which the input keeps and its derived `visibility-button` toggles, never an event of a flow and always enabled; the screen's independent areas are the regions of a parallel state, each event handled by one area, a region's state leaving for a state of the root by `#<flow id>.<state>`, the flow's `id` a one-segment name the flow declares, which the app gives its machine; an event is an anchor acted on, a platform event, or a data event `model.<entity>.<field>` of a field the screen shows; a control whose anchor is an event (a button, a select, a switch) is enabled exactly in the states that handle it, read from the machine, never from a flag), and `contract:check` runs every flow and activity in XState, while an app holds its own machine for a screen to its flow with the engine's comparison (the web app's `apps/web/src/platform/machines.spec.ts`). A screen file may write its flow shorter, a transition as its target, an effect by its name (`show: <anchor>`), a region by its pattern (`draft`) or by its button (`by: <button>`), which the loader expands into that config before anything reads it (`bun run contract:flow <screen>` prints it); a shape one region has is written in full until a second shares it. **What the contract says elsewhere, a screen leaves out**, and the loader derives it the same way every time (`contract/README.md`, "What a screen leaves out"): what an action fails with (the platform's codes; for the model, `forbidden` under `x-perch.can`, the codes of the constraints of the fields it sets, `failed`), an error's `codes` and `fields` from the actions its part binds, the codes a `show: <anchor>` in an `onError` shows, and the whole region of a button bound to one action that the flow does not handle. The screen writes only what a tester or a user can tell apart; a written value wins over a derived one, and `contract:diff` lists the codes the anchors gain or lose, derived ones included.
- **Activities.** A task across screens is an activity, `contract/domain/activities/<id>.yaml`: an XState machine config whose states are visits to screens (a dot-free name, `meta.screen`, `meta.address` a binding of that screen) and whose transitions on an anchor of that screen or the frame the screens' flows or links must back, which a scenario that walks it names in its `activity`.
- **The checks**, in `bun run check` and the pre-commit hook: `contract:check` (the files, their cross-references, the schema drift, the scenarios' brackets), `contract:check:sources` (the JSX of `apps/web/src` and `packages/ui/src`), `contract:check:consumers` (the literals of `tests/e2e/src`). On a branch the hook lets the contract run ahead of the app (`contract:check:sources:ahead`: the app uses only what the contract declares, and an anchor declared but not rendered yet passes; and the unit tests with `CONTRACT_AHEAD=1`: an app's machine may lack what its flow added, a state, an event, a guarded branch, an effect, but does nothing the flow does not say, or is still exactly its screen's flow at the branch's merge base with `main` (`CONTRACT_AHEAD_BASE`), so a business commit may change a transition the app has not changed yet; and a scenario may wait for its test, as the hook leaves `e2e:coverage` out); a commit on `main` and `bun run check` take the full check, `e2e:coverage` included, so nothing lands unrendered, half-built or untested. A rename or a removal therefore goes in three commits: the business commit adds the new name and keeps the old one (the review stop), the implementation switches to it, a last business commit drops the old one, approved with the first. Coverage measures the model first, then the controls; a control off it carries `// ui-contract-ignore[-self]: <reason>` or `{/* ui-contract-ignore[-self]: <reason> */}` right before its element.
- **The manifest** is built only after the check passes and served by every stand at `/ui-manifest/manifest.json` (`PERCH_UI_MANIFEST=0` turns it off); development and e2e builds add `window.__perch.ui.anchors()`. A contract change hands over `bun run contract:diff main` with the reason for each rename or removal.
